{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-223358","model_call":24,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ watermelons (+ \"supplies are down\" \"supply is down\" \"lower supplies\" \"reduced supply\") (+ because due weather drought rain harvest))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ watermelons (+ \"supplies are down\" \"supply is down\" \"lower supplies\" \"reduced supply\") (+ because due weather drought rain harvest))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_564957766#12_1257215444","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2748","rank":1,"snippet":"\" , \"segment\": \"Factories produced fewer fidget-spinners, and businesses started marking down their inventory to sell through their remaining fidget spinners. Another example of the laws of supply and demand is a seasonal fruit. Most fruits only grow during specific parts of the year. For example, watermelons are a summer fruit, so the majority of melons are harvested during the summer months. During the summer, supply is high, so it is easy to purchase watermelons at a reasonable price. If you want to buy <cover>watermelons out of season, your options are more scarce, and you’re likely to pay more because of the reduced supply. </cover>What is the law of demand? The law of demand states that the price of a good plays a significant role in determining consumer demand for that good. All else being equal, the more something costs, the fewer consumers will buy that good. The lower a goods’ price, the higher the purchases for that good will be.\" , \"start_char\": 4556.000000 , \"title\": \"What is the Law of Supply and Demand? - 2020 - Robinhood\" , \"url\":"},"time":7794},"model_calls":24,"document_judgments":17,"relevance_3":0,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
