{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-223358","model_call":86,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ watermelon (+ out-of-season \"out of season\" seasonal seasonality) (+ shortage scarce expensive supply))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ watermelon (+ out-of-season \"out of season\" seasonal seasonality) (+ shortage scarce expensive supply))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_547171863#0_1274523889","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2808","rank":1,"snippet":"\"segment\": \"5 Seasonal Fruits and Vegetables for Spring 5 Seasonal Fruits and Vegetables for Spring April 13, 2014 By Spoon University I know you can barely hold your excitement, but you must first understand what it means to eat seasonally. Just like how eating bread freshly baked in the morning supersedes the ones left on the shelf on sale, produce at its peak during the year has a much higher quality and bargain. Seasonal produce is cheaper and more abundant than <cover>out-of-season ingredients. Thanks to technology, we have unlimited access to different foods. But watermelon in the middle of February is not only tasteless; it’s more expensive, </cover>has a larger carbon footprint and isn’t as nutrient-dense as it is when it’s freshly picked in the summer. Fruits and vegetables taste so much better when picked fresh and when it’s not shipped from the other side of the globe frozen in a box for days. So here are some amazingly delicious picks for spring! 1. Asparagus This stalky green is harvested from March to June and is high in vitamin C, vitamin A and folate.\" , \"start_char"},"time":1929},"model_calls":86,"document_judgments":43,"relevance_3":0,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
